## Invariants

Changing one takes an ADR and the owner's sign-off.

1. **One fact, one tier.** A fact lives at the narrowest tier that fully owns it. T0 is the org tier
   (`~/.claude/skills/`), T1 this repository, T2 a code repo's own `CLAUDE.md`. Lower tiers
   reference, never copy. Tie-breaker: **co-locate by change cadence, not by topic.** If it changes
   when a token rotates it belongs with the mechanics; if it changes when the process changes it
   belongs with the policy.
2. **A normative rule is stated once.** Every other mention is a reference. A hard rule copied into a
   second file is a drift hazard, not reinforcement.
3. **No claim without provenance.** Every non-obvious claim carries a resolvable token (below). An
   orphan claim is a lint failure, not a style note.
4. **Propose, then ask, then apply.** No agent mutates this repository's knowledge without per-item
   human approval, and every applied change is logged.
5. **Append-only history.** ADRs are amended, never rewritten; the rejected path is the record. State
   records are replaced on re-verification, with the prior version in git history, not in the file.
6. **Committable by construction.** Anything that cannot be committed (secrets, personal contact
   data, regulated content) is not knowledge-repo material. Knowledge MUST NOT be gitignored.
7. **No normative claim depends on a target outside this repository.** A `[[wikilink]]` carries
   supplementary depth or deliberately-external private data only.

## Provenance tokens

Cited inline, at the end of the sentence. `.claude/hooks/provenance-lint.sh` resolves every token;
one that does not resolve is a lint failure.

| Form | Means |
|---|---|
| `[src: adr/0012]`, `[src: adr/0012#am2]` | a decision record, or one of its amendments |
| `[src: state/<slug>@YYYY-MM-DD]` | a dated live-state record |
| `[src: contract/<slug>@X.Y.Z]` | a versioned seam contract |
| `[src: domain/<slug>#<anchor>]` | a definition in `docs/domain/` |
| `[src: repo/<alias>:<path>]`, `[src: repo/<alias>:<path>:<lines>]` | a code location; `<alias>` is a `MANIFEST.yaml: repos` alias |
| `[verified: YYYY-MM-DD]` | a first-hand observation with no file to cite |

These five `src:` productions are the closed set. The `repo/` prefix on a code reference is
**required**; the bare `<alias>:<path>` form is retired and lints as a legacy spelling. A code
reference with no `@commit-ish` resolves against that repository's `repos[].pinned_at`, which is
declared **once** in `MANIFEST.yaml` rather than repeated in every token; advancing that pin is
itself a curation act. Write `@<commit-ish>` in a token only for a claim about a historical state
the pin no longer names.

A `verified:` token anchors in time, not to a file, so it never joins the decay graph and **SHOULD**
become a state record. Tokens containing `<`, `>` or `...` are grammar, not citations, and are
ignored, so this table does not report itself as a defect.

## Modal vocabulary

Every normative line carries exactly one of **MUST**, **MUST NOT**, **SHOULD**, **SHOULD NOT**,
**MAY**. An unmarked line is context and is not enforced as a rule.

## Curation (always on)

The procedure is the T0 skill `bone-curator` and is not restated here. There is no T1 wrapper: a
thin wrapper would be a second name for one procedure, and a unit that is not project-specific may
not sit in the project capability directory.

- `.claude/hooks/decay-check.sh` reports the Context Decay Index with its coverage at session start.
  **If it reports staleness, run the audit and propose changes before other work.** Also run it when
  a decision locks, a contract version changes, or a state record is re-verified.
- Changeset verbs are the closed set **ADD / UPDATE / REMOVE / MERGE / SPLIT**.
- Propose, ask per item, apply approved only, log to `CURATION.md` plus a record in `docs/curation/`.
  A direct instruction from the owner is a valid sign-off **only when it names the target artifact
  and the verb**, and it MUST be quoted verbatim in the record. Refutations are logged too, so they
  are not re-proposed.
- **One event, one commit.** An applied curation lands as a single commit carrying both the changed
  artifacts and its record. A commit that edits governed knowledge and adds no record in
  `docs/curation/` is an unattested apply.
- **No skill may contradict this file, an accepted ADR, or a current state record.**

## Gates

The gate set is enumerated **once**, in `MANIFEST.yaml: gates`; every other mention references a gate
by id. A checklist absent from that list is not a gate.

Verdicts are the closed set `PASS` / `FAIL` / `NEEDS-INFO`. Every item reports a verdict, a locator
(`file:line` or resource id) and, on failure, a one-line remediation. **Any FAIL blocks the merge.**
`NEEDS-INFO` blocks until resolved: it exists so an under-determined item cannot become a false pass.

## Security

**MUST NOT** write a secret value, personal contact data, private key or customer data into any file
here; reference secrets by store and name. On finding a leak, **MUST** report rather than repair:
state the location, do not reproduce the value, open a rotation item.

## House style

- No em-dash (U+2014) in **any** text file here, including shell scripts and YAML.
- Status and verdicts live in table cells or front matter. Headings are stable identifiers and MUST
  NOT carry mutable state.
- Skill references use the backticked bare name (`` `{{PREFIX}}-context` ``). The `/name` form is
  reserved for commands that exist.
- This file MUST stay under 8 KB (roughly 2k tokens). It is loaded in full by every session, so its
  size is a tax paid constantly. Anything longer is a pointer, not a constitution. Measure the
  substituted file, not the template: the project name expands.

The admission rule for new `docs/` subdirectories is stated once, in `MANIFEST.yaml: docs_registry`.
